WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.700
Actor-critic.

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:08.133
Not one algorithm, but the two-part template under A2C, A3C, PPO, and SAC.

3
00:00:09.333 --> 00:00:10.864
The actor is the policy.

4
00:00:10.864 --> 00:00:12.267
It decides what to do.

5
00:00:12.567 --> 00:00:14.256
The critic estimates values.

6
00:00:14.256 --> 00:00:16.067
It judges how good a state is.

7
00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:20.033
In practice it is one network with two heads.

8
00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:23.967
The observation goes into a shared trunk that extracts features.

9
00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:26.367
The actor head outputs the policy.

10
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:29.333
And the critic head outputs the value of the state.

11
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:31.933
Why bother with a critic?

12
00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:34.613
Raw returns are astronomically noisy.

13
00:00:34.613 --> 00:00:37.700
A good action in a doomed episode gets punished.

14
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.067
So we compare what we got, the reward plus the next value, to what we expected from here.

15
00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:45.475
That difference is the advantage.

16
00:00:45.475 --> 00:00:48.733
Subtracting expectations cancels most of the noise.

17
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:51.867
Here is that network in PyTorch.

18
00:00:52.167 --> 00:00:53.300
One shared body.

19
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.733
The actor is a linear layer producing action logits.

20
00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:59.600
The critic produces just one number, the value.

21
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:06.033
Open Stable Baselines3 and find the actor and critic heads in the policies file.

22
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:10.067
Then find where the advantage is computed in the PPO file.

23
00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:15.043
In RLHF, the value head bolted onto the LLM is the critic.

24
00:01:15.043 --> 00:01:18.833
Same template, seventy billion parameter actor.

25
00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:23.267
The mistake is learning from raw episode returns alone.

26
00:01:23.567 --> 00:01:25.648
Add a critic baseline instead.

27
00:01:25.648 --> 00:01:29.533
Actions with positive advantage go up, negative go down.

28
00:01:30.733 --> 00:01:31.509
Let's recap.

29
00:01:31.509 --> 00:01:34.933
The actor is the policy, the critic estimates values.

30
00:01:35.233 --> 00:01:38.100
The advantage asks: was that better than expected?

31
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.467
It cancels noise and speeds learning.

32
00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:44.013
This template sits under PPO and its cousins.

33
00:01:44.013 --> 00:01:46.033
Next up: deep RL in general.
